.TH DIFF 1 2026-09-25 "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
diff \- compare files line by line
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B diff
.RB [ \-c | \-C
.IR n | \fB\-u\fP | \fB\-U\fP
.IR n | \fB\-e\fP | \fB\-f\fP | \fB\-n\fP | \fB\-q\fP | \fB\-\-normal\fP ]
.RB [ \-abirstwBN ]
.RB [ \-I
.IR regexp ]
.RB [ \-L
.IR label ]
.I file1 file2
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B diff
compares
.I file1
and
.I file2
and writes a description of the changes needed to turn
.I file1
into
.IR file2 .
Either operand may be
.B \-
for standard input.
.PP
The comparison uses the Myers O(ND) shortest-edit-script algorithm.
If the two files are so large and dissimilar that the algorithm's
working memory would exceed about 128 MiB,
.B diff
instead reports every line as changed; the output is still a correct
edit script, but not a minimal one.
Input files of 1 GiB or more are rejected.
.PP
A file containing a NUL byte is treated as binary unless
.B \-a
is given.
If either file is binary, only the line
.BI "Binary files " file1 " and " file2 " differ"
is printed when they differ.
.PP
A last line without a trailing newline never matches a
newline-terminated line, and is followed in the output by
.BR "\e No newline at end of file" .
.SS Directories
If exactly one operand is a directory,
.B diff
compares the other file with the file of the same base name in that
directory.
If both are directories, entries are compared in sorted name order:
an entry present in only one directory is reported as
.BI "Only in " dir ": " name \fR,\fP
a pair of subdirectories as
.BI "Common subdirectories: " a " and " b
(unless
.B \-r
is given), and a pair of files is compared.
When a file and a directory of the same name meet, an error is
reported.
.SS Output formats
.TP
Normal (default)
Change commands of the form
.IB range1 a range2\fR,\fP
.IB range1 c range2\fR,\fP
and
.IB range1 d range2\fR,\fP
followed by the affected lines prefixed with
.B <
(from
.IR file1 )
and
.B >
(from
.IR file2 ),
with
.B \-\-\-
between the two groups of a change.
.TP
Context (\fB\-c\fP)
Hunks of changed lines with surrounding context, marked with
.B !
(changed),
.B \-
(deleted), and
.B +
(added), under a
.B ***
/
.B \-\-\-
file header.
.TP
Unified (\fB\-u\fP)
Hunks headed by
.BI "@@ \-" "start,count" " +" "start,count" " @@"
with lines prefixed by a space (context),
.BR \- ,
or
.BR + ,
under a
.B \-\-\-
/
.B +++
file header.
.TP
Ed script (\fB\-e\fP)
.BR ed (1)
commands, in reverse line order, that turn
.I file1
into
.IR file2 .
.TP
Forward (\fB\-f\fP)
Like
.BR \-e ,
but in forward order with the command letter before the line
numbers.
.TP
RCS (\fB\-n\fP)
RCS-style
\fBd\fP\fIline\fP\ \fIcount\fP
and
\fBa\fP\fIline\fP\ \fIcount\fP
commands, each
.B a
followed by the lines to add.
.PP
In the context and unified formats, the file headers show only the
file name (or label); no timestamps are printed.
Hunks separated by no more than twice the context length are merged.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Output format
.TP
.B \-\-normal
Normal output (the default).
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-context [= \fIn\fP ]
Context output with 3 lines (or
.IR n )
of context.
.TP
.BI \-C " n"
Context output with
.I n
lines of context.
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-unified [= \fIn\fP ]
Unified output with 3 lines (or
.IR n )
of context.
.TP
.BI \-U " n"
Unified output with
.I n
lines of context.
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-ed
Ed script output.
.TP
.B \-f
Forward ed-style output.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-rcs
RCS output.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-brief
Only report
.BI "Files " file1 " and " file2 " differ"
when the files differ.
.PP
The last format option given takes effect.
Context lengths must be between 0 and 1048576.
.SS Comparison
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-text
Treat all files as text.
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-ignore\-space\-change
Treat any run of white space as a single blank, and ignore trailing
white space.
.TP
.BR \-w ", " \-\-ignore\-all\-space
Ignore all white space.
Takes precedence over
.BR \-b .
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-ignore\-case
Ignore case differences.
.TP
.BR \-B ", " \-\-ignore\-blank\-lines
Suppress changes in which every inserted and deleted line is blank
(empty or white space only).
.TP
.BI \-I " regexp"
Suppress changes in which every inserted and deleted line matches the
extended regular expression
.IR regexp .
.SS Other options
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-recursive
Compare subdirectories recursively (to a depth of 64).
In normal, context, and unified formats, each pair of compared files
is preceded by a line
.BI "diff " "file1 file2" \fR.\fP
.TP
.BR \-N ", " \-\-new\-file
Treat a missing file, or a file present in only one directory, as an
empty file
.RB ( /dev/null ).
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-report\-identical\-files
Report
.BI "Files " file1 " and " file2 " are identical"
when they are.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-expand\-tabs
Expand tabs to spaces (8-column stops) in output lines of the normal,
context, and unified formats.
.TP
.BR \-L ", " \-\-label= \fIlabel\fP
Use
.I label
instead of the file name in the context or unified header.
The first
.B \-L
labels
.IR file1 ,
the second
.IR file2 .
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print version information and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
No differences were found.
.TP
.B 1
Differences were found.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid usage or an error (unreadable file, bad regular expression,
file/directory mismatch, directory nesting too deep).
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /dev/null
Stands in for a missing file with
.BR \-N .
.SH EXAMPLES
Produce a unified diff suitable for a patch:
.PP
.EX
$ diff \-u old.c new.c > change.diff
.EE
.PP
List which files differ between two trees:
.PP
.EX
$ diff \-rq dir1 dir2
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cmp (1),
.BR ed (1),
.BR regex (3)
.SH STANDARDS
POSIX.1-2024 for
.BR \-b ,
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-C ,
.BR \-e ,
.BR \-f ,
.BR \-r ,
.BR \-u ,
and
.BR \-U ;
the other options follow GNU diffutils and BSD.
.SH BUGS
Context and unified headers carry no file timestamps.
.PP
With
.BR \-e ,
.BR \-f ,
or
.BR \-n ,
an inserted line consisting of a single
.B .
is not escaped, so the resulting script is wrong.
.PP
When both
.B \-B
and
.B \-I
are given, only
.B \-B
is applied.
.PP
With
.B \-N
in a directory comparison, a subdirectory present on only one side is
skipped silently.
